{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it","id":196085,"title":"On the usage of \"she\" instead of \"it\"","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-07-13T00:32:41-07:00","id":196085,"title":"On the usage of \"she\" instead of \"it\"","content":"Could someone explain me the rule behind it? I see it frequently, but as I really like English (not my mothertongue), I'd like to know when to use it and when not to.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blood &amp; Smoke, Protean</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b> Rubbery Flesh: </b>Warping <u>her</u> flesh into a rubbery substance and her bones into flexible cartilage, the vampire can stretch, condense, and contort <u>her</u> body in impossible ways. <u>She</u> can fit through almost any gap more than an inch (two centimeters) across. If the contortion requires a roll \u2014 such as squeezing through a drain cover while someone\u2019s shooting at <u>her</u> \u2014 add the vampire\u2019s Protean dots to <u>her</u> dice pool.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/316-jesuncolo","name":"Jesuncolo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar316_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T00:52:41-07:00","id":196096,"title":"","content":"Describing someone as 'it' is impersonal and can be read as rude. English doesn't have a good singular, gender-neutral pronoun and using 'they' and 'their' as the singular is clumsy.<br /> <br /> In Onyx Path products, the writers try to personalise the writing by using 'he' or 'she' in examples when referring to an unnamed example person - from your quote, 'the vampire' character could be male or female, but the description sounds nicer as 'warping her flesh' than 'warping its flesh', or 'It can fit through, or 'shooting at it', or 'Protean dots to its dice pool'.<br /> <br /> Writers I've worked with try to vary which gender is used between examples, but as we're working on individual sections and don't know as we're writing how they'll flow with all the other drafts, we trust in the editors and developers to make the usage relatively even.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T02:35:01-07:00","id":196161,"title":"","content":"&quot;It&quot; implies not-living, so it's only good for fashionably creepy people (you can call me &quot;it&quot;). &quot;They&quot; is 100% proper grammar to use for a single person; Some people are buggy about it, but there has always been a lot of good grammar that bugs people. If I don' use &quot;they,&quot; I'm more likely to use &quot;she&quot; than &quot;he&quot; because always going with &quot;he&quot; is the historical problem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/486-foolish-milex","name":"Foolish Milex","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar486_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T04:52:02-07:00","id":196192,"title":"","content":"Better alternating between he and she and using 'xir' madness that started to infest English language, I will get lynched by tumblr social justice warriors, but 'xir' is like defiling English language for sake of nebulous political correctness. <br /> <br /> I'm totally fine with using he and she here and there, for the sake of variety.<br /> <br /> It I would only reserve for beings so detached for humanity that calling such beings in other way would be useless. Your average vampire is still a human in a way, just methabolically challenged.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T06:20:21-07:00","id":196229,"title":"","content":"Just to add, It is common that the male pronouns are used in writing. However I believe that Onyx Path has continued the practiced that White Wolf did when choosing to use the female pronouns instead. Neither is incorrect (as far as I know).<br /> <br /> And vampires are always an &quot;it&quot; to me. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T07:00:28-07:00","id":196249,"title":"","content":"Well, in my language it's male gendered, but again my language is gender marked in general. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T07:34:46-07:00","id":196268,"title":"","content":"So there is no hard-fast rule. It's just &quot;because I don't like to use it for people&quot;?<br /> <br /> Vampires in my tongue are male gendered, as well as everything non-gendered, but I thought that everything that is non-gendered in English was always referred to as &quot;it&quot;. It's not an Onyx Path exclusive either: it seldom happened to me that a D&amp;D book referring to a &quot;spellcaster&quot; as &quot;she&quot;, though the caster itself was not gendered.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/316-jesuncolo","name":"Jesuncolo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar316_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T07:45:37-07:00","id":196275,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jesuncolo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=196268#post196268\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So there is no hard-fast rule. It's just &quot;because I don't like to use it for people?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Unless the rules have changed, a person of unknown gender is to be referred to with male pronouns. An increasing number of people will ignore this rule because the rule is kind of dumb, replacing it with plural pronouns (annoying), &quot;he or she&quot; (clunky, but whatever), or, in the seemingly unique case of RPG books, by alternating pronouns every time the text switches cases; I'll guarantee the next power that uses pronouns will say &quot;he,&quot; and the one after that will say &quot;she&quot; again.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jesuncolo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=196268#post196268\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Vampires in my tongue are male gendered, as well as everything non-gendered, but I thought that everything that is non-gendered in English was always referred to as &quot;it.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Only inanimate objects and usually animals. &quot;It&quot; is considered an offensive term. For an example of this, find a place to watch the <b>South Park</b> episode &quot;With Apologies to Jesse Jackson,&quot; in which Cartman continually refers to a little person as an it because he doesn't consider the little person to be human.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T07:54:09-07:00","id":196298,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=196275#post196275\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unless the rules have changed, a person of unknown gender is to be referred to with male pronouns.</div> </div> </div> </div>It still is in English, but as mentioned above by Shadowflame (#5) Onyx Path is continuing on with White wolf tradition of alternating between &quot;He&quot; and &quot;she&quot;<br /> in their Stories (covering both the (un)life of male and female vampire).<br /> <br /> At least in French we have a seperate now for both male and female vampire.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Project consolidation:<br /> Rough Draft: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1230422-rough-idea-for-a-werewolf-mini-supplement\" target=\"_blank\">Dhole Shifters</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1212511-city-book-seeking-advice\" target=\"_blank\">Ottawa By Night</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1194394-fanon-tribebook-bunyip-wip\" target=\"_blank\">Tribebook Bunyip</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/353881-your-new-revamped-tribes?p=354143#post354143\" target=\"_blank\">Garou Variant</a><br /> In redaction: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1151956-%E1%B9%A3ill%C4%81nu-b%C4%93lu-lasombra-bloodline-feedback-request\" target=\"_blank\">Lasombra Bloodline</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3226-boneguard","name":"Boneguard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3226_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"477"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T08:20:00-07:00","id":196308,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boneguard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=196298#post196298\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It still is in English, but as mentioned above by Shadowflame (#5) Onyx Path is continuing on with White Wolf tradition of alternating between &quot;he&quot; and &quot;she&quot;<br /> in their stories.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know. I referred to that in the same paragraph you quoted.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T08:45:18-07:00","id":196314,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jesuncolo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=196268#post196268\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So there is no hard-fast rule. It's just &amp;quot;because I don't like to use it for people&amp;quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div> No, &quot;it&quot; is only used for objects, not subjects. People are either &quot;he&quot; or &quot;she&quot;, and technically &quot;he&quot; doubles as the grammatically correct gender-neutral term for people. However, that rule is pretty out dated, as it doesn't take a genius to realize that &quot;he&quot; is a gendered pronoun. Some people use &quot;they&quot;, and this is technically incorrect because &quot;they&quot; is plural, but it is generally accepted because for one thing, not all native English speakers know all of the rules of our language, and because it's preferable to the unfortunate implications of the male gendered pronoun being used as the default for people of unspecified gender. &quot;He or she&quot; is technically correct, but is very clunky. &quot;One&quot; correct in some situations, i.e. &quot; Warping one's flesh and one's bones into flexible cartilage&quot; is fine, but &quot;the vampire can stretch, condense, and contort one's body in impossible ways&quot; doesn't work. There isn't really a gender-neutral pronoun that works in all situations besides &quot;it&quot; which objectifies the subject, so there is a lot of inconsistency between different writers. There was some effort to introduce a singular, gender-neutral subject pronoun to the language (xir, as Katsura mentioned), but it was largely unsuccessful, in no small part because &quot;xir&quot; just sounds silly. White Wolf and Onyx Path alternate between &quot;he&quot; and &quot;she&quot; every time the subject changes, which is in my opinion the best solution, as it is entirely grammatically correct. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Vampires in my tongue are male gendered, as well as everything non-gendered, but I thought that everything that is non-gendered in English was always referred to as &amp;quot;it&amp;quot;. It's not an Onyx Path exclusive either: it seldom happened to me that a D&amp;D book referring to a &amp;quot;spellcaster&amp;quot; as &amp;quot;she&amp;quot;, though the caster itself was not gendered. </div> </div> </div> Every thing is referred to as &quot;it&quot; in English, but people are not things. As a vampire and a spellcaster are both kinds of people, referring to either as &quot;it&quot; is objectifying.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T09:04:06-07:00","id":196321,"title":"","content":"I always used &quot;it&quot; for gender-neutral. But I understand that could lead to dissonance when referring to someone who falls inbetween or none.<br /> Ok, so it always refers to objects unless you want to objectify a person or call it inhuman. I should mostly use &quot;he&quot; or the impersonal &quot;one&quot; for gender-neutral, though they both feel a little wrong. Am I right up to this point?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/316-jesuncolo","name":"Jesuncolo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar316_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T09:41:16-07:00","id":196358,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jesuncolo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=196321#post196321\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I always used &amp;quot;it&amp;quot; for gender-neutral. But I understand that could lead to dissonance when referring to someone who falls inbetween or none. Ok, so it always refers to objects unless you want to objectify a person or call it inhuman. I should mostly use &amp;quot;he&amp;quot; or the impersonal &amp;quot;one&amp;quot; for gender-neutral, though they both feel a little wrong. Am I right up to this point?</div> </div> </div> </div> Correct. &quot;They&quot; is also a sort of informal option. Even though it's supposed to be plural, people use it in the singular all the time in casual conversation, and it's essentially part if the modern lexicon at this point, though I still wouldn't use it on a formal written piece.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T11:07:25-07:00","id":196439,"title":"","content":"A bit of important linguistic history:<br /> <br /> The singular they is first recorded as being used in the 1400s.<br /> <br /> The gender-neutral he was actively started as a movement in the 1750s, and wasn't commonly accepted until later.<br /> <br /> As English has no official body of authority for what counts as officially correct (which most languages do have), we're stuck with something we've been using for over 600 years or something that some academics convinced the British parliament to start using a bit over 200 years ago. There's no actual way to say which is &quot;more grammatically correct,&quot; because there's actually no such thin in English besides popular usage. But the singular they has both the longer standing usage (it has been part of English before the full transition to modern standardized English was finished), and is actually neutral.<br /> <br /> Also, an important alternative that often gets ignored is &quot;one&quot; which can function as a personal gender-neutral pronoun. However one, just like they and he, runs into some issues in terms of both flow and formality.<br /> <br /> Middle English did have a gender neutral pronoun but for whatever reason we dropped it. However one of the better invented pronouns for this is an attempt to modernize said dropped pronoun. Which basically involves dropped the &quot;th&quot; from the &quot;they&quot; equivalents since the older one essentially worked on the same principle. Though in some dialects it isn't all that clear.<br /> <br /> --------<br /> <br /> Anyway, the WW/OP approach of variation in using he and she is a very useful compromise position. By mixing up both you avoid the inclusive issue, avoid the language flow issue of using they all the time or having to constantly establish the gender of a vampire in every example, and avoid the tone issue of using one all the time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T07:04:54-07:00","id":196753,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=196439#post196439\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A bit of important linguistic history<br /> <br /> --------<br /> <br /> Anyway, the WW/OP approach of variation in using he and she is a very useful compromise position. By mixing up both you avoid the inclusive issue, avoid the language flow issue of using they all the time or having to constantly establish the gender of a vampire in every example, and avoid the tone issue of using one all the time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Since English is so adept at stealing words from other languages, I recommend using &quot;ta&quot;. &quot;ta&quot; is Chinese for he/she. The Chinese language did not differentiate gender until the British imposed it on their written records during their Imperial days.<br /> <br /> In the VTES CCG, WW cleverly used &quot;she&quot; describe when a player does things like play Master cards or Influence Minions and &quot;he&quot; to describe when a player does things like play Minion cards or take Minion actions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T07:14:54-07:00","id":196755,"title":"","content":"Communication and understanding is supposed to be the rule of thumb. That's why even those that have a wide vocabulary won't use $50 words.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T09:07:47-07:00","id":196838,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=196314#post196314\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> No, &quot;it&quot; is only used for objects, not subjects.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this statement is problematic when talking about grammar. In the sentence &quot;It fell to the floor.&quot;, &quot;it&quot; is clearly the subject.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Dex Davican wrote:</b> I can say without exaggeration or dishonesty that I am the most creative man <i>ever to have lived</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/75-weimann","name":"Weimann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar75_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1279"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T09:33:23-07:00","id":196862,"title":"","content":"I think the qualifier here is that when an object is the subject, then &quot;it&quot; is appropriate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T10:06:39-07:00","id":196927,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Since English is so adept at stealing words from other languages, I recommend using &quot;ta&quot;. &quot;ta&quot; is Chinese for he/she. The Chinese language did not differentiate gender until the British imposed it on their written records during their Imperial days. </div> </div> </div>It steals only words that are secondary to most basic words which English inherited from its Germanic ancestors. Replacing pronouns with some foreign would be a sacrilege.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T11:41:23-07:00","id":197061,"title":"","content":"Can we... drop the hyperbolic descriptors of changes that you don't like?<br /> <br /> English doesn't have a board regulating its linguistic evolution. There's no rules to violate in terms of what different dialects of English adopt as being appropriate or not.<br /> <br /> Adding in a word from a very distant language can be argued against without words like &quot;sacrilege.&quot; Simply pointing out that the precursor language to modern English <i>had</i> the pronoun we're looking for, and many modern languages from the same language family as English also maintained a personal gender neutral pronoun, thus they are easier to adopt to modern English is plenty of argument for why picking Chinese is not a good idea in this instance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T12:26:56-07:00","id":197092,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Weimann</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=196838#post196838\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think this statement is problematic when talking about grammar. In the sentence &amp;quot;It fell to the floor.&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;it&amp;quot; is clearly the subject.</div> </div> </div> </div> Good point. I guess it's just not meant to describe humans.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T12:35:03-07:00","id":197098,"title":"","content":"I think I need a bucket and mop. My head is close to exploding...somewhere in this thread.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T15:11:32-07:00","id":197222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=196314#post196314\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> No, &quot;it&quot; is only used for objects, not subjects.</div> </div> </div> </div> Different usage of the word object than subject/object.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T17:48:26-07:00","id":197285,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nobody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=197222#post197222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Different usage of the word object than subject/object.</div> </div> </div> </div> Yeah, my bad there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-15T05:14:39-07:00","id":197809,"title":"","content":"Others have said it better than me, but I'll give it a go.<br /> <br /> As mentioned previously, Old English used to have a different set of pronouns:<br /> <br /> <b>h\u0113, hit, h\u0113o</b><br /> <br /> which evolved into:<br /> <br /> <b>he, it, she</b> (technically, she did not evolve from <b>h\u0113o</b>)<br /> <br /> Originally, as in German, these would have mainly been used in reference to the grammatical gender of the noun. As English lost gender on nouns, <b>he</b> and <b>she</b> became animate pronouns (for the two genders) where as <b>it</b> became an inanimate pronoun. (This is different from a neuter pronoun).<br /> <br /> So the problem is that <b>it</b> (except in special cases, such as a child) refers to an inanimate object and using it on a human being is seen as insulting and depersonalising. Very early after the formation of Middle English, a gender neutral animate pronoun <b>they</b> came into being. Of course this is problematic as it is identical to the third person plural pronoun <b>they</b>, similar to the Standard English feature of the second person pronoun, <b>you</b>, being identical in the singular and plural.<br /> <br /> As mentioned above, <b>he</b> was declared to be a gender neutral pronoun around the mid-18th century, but:<br /> <br /> (a) This declaration doesn't reflect the historical facts of the language or the usage of many native speakers and good writers.<br /> (b) It is seen as having sexist implications to refer to any unnamed human being as a <b>he</b>.<br /> <br /> So when writing in a context where the character has no particular gender one can:<br /> <br /> (a) Use <b>he</b>, a recent usage and of questionable implications.<br /> (b) Use <b>they</b>, the older usage.<br /> (c) Alternate <b>he</b> and <b>she</b>, uncommon but grammatically correct.<br /> <br /> The difference between the last two is mostly stylistic. Continually using <b>they</b> can be a bit dry and perhaps not as &quot;vital&quot; as <b>he</b> and <b>she</b>. I think the usage of alternating <b>he</b> and <b>she</b> for RPG books is a good practice, as in RPGs one is often imagining and playing a vividly realised character and a generic <b>they</b> can be a bit colourless. <br /> <br /> For example, to me, <b>she</b> implies sentences like:<br /> <br /> <i>Jessica storms into the Elysium, her muscles ready to snap the Sheriff's neck, she has been preparing for this.</i><br /> <br /> where as <b>they</b> implies:<br /> <br /> <i>The character enters the Elysium and they activate Vigour in preparation for combat with the Sheriff.</i><br /> <br /> Of course this is an exaggeration, but hopefully it conveys the stylistic difference.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3326-an-fhuiseog","name":"An Fhuiseog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3326_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"271"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-15T16:11:51-07:00","id":198301,"title":"","content":"Hmm. Interesting. I did not know that history quite like that. Thank you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-15T16:12:21-07:00","id":198303,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/196085-on-the-usage-of-she-instead-of-it?p=197285#post197285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Yeah, my bad there.</div> </div> </div> </div>Tis ok. =)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2058-nobody","name":"Nobody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}